WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:06.049
Dr. Bradshaw had only been working at the
SCP Foundation for a few years, but in that

00:00:06.049 --> 00:00:09.300
time, he had seen things that had changed
him irreparably.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:14.870
Ancient texts about world-ending abominations,
machines that could spontaneously produce

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:20.730
any food or drink from nowhere, friendly slime
monsters with a penchant for tickling, invisible

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:25.550
kleptomaniac women and wannabe-heroic men
covered in spikes.

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:31.230
But one anomaly, in particular, stuck with
him, haunted his dreams, and dominated his

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:35.360
waking thoughts: SCP-049.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:40.770
That mysterious masked doctor, with his deadly
touch and strange beaked visage, trying in

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:48.399
vain to cure something he would only refer
to as "The Pestilence" and "The Great Dying."

00:00:48.399 --> 00:00:53.740
His "cure" was more like a curse, it seemed,
as his unusual treatments transformed his

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:59.399
patients into the mindless living dead, shuffling
around as malformed shells of their former

00:00:59.399 --> 00:01:00.399
selves.

00:01:00.399 --> 00:01:06.420
If this was the cure, Dr. Bradshaw found himself
often wondering, what was the disease?

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:11.210
Many researchers had attempted to get to the
bottom of this question over the years, interviewing

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:17.240
SCP-049 and nurturing their own pet theories
in their free time, but none had ever received

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:21.600
a satisfactory answer, ever reached a real
conclusion.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.460
It was this legacy of fruitless hypothesizing
and questioning that led Dr. Bradshaw to pitch

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.640
his own plan for unmasking the Pestilence
once and for all.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:35.180
He would gather every prominent theory his
colleagues could come up with, including several

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:36.180
of his own.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:41.360
Then, he would simply engage the Plague Doctor
in polite discourse, walking him through each

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:45.719
theory one by one and waiting to see if any
of them stuck.

00:01:45.719 --> 00:01:50.590
It took Dr. Bradshaw weeks to collect and
distill the theories down into a workable

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:55.210
list of questions, consolidating those that
overlapped and eliminating those that were

00:01:55.210 --> 00:02:00.799
simply ridiculous (such as Dr. Bright's potentially
facetious insistence that the Pestilence was

00:02:00.799 --> 00:02:07.070
lactose intolerance), but when the day came,
he felt ready to take on the task.

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:11.140
When the interview was through, perhaps he
would have managed to do what none before

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:13.610
him at the Foundation had.

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:18.019
Perhaps he would understand what the Plague
Doctor was fighting with his unorthodox methods,

00:02:18.019 --> 00:02:23.450
and by extension, how they could pacify him
non-lethally, once and for all.

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:28.250
Dr. Bradshaw held his notebook tight as he
made the long walk down the hall from his

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:31.340
office to SCP-049's containment room.

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:36.220
This was it, the moment of truth...he hoped.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:40.500
He had asked his colleagues to pump the scent
of lavender into 049's room to put him at

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:44.380
ease, as well as give him his dinner just
in time for the meeting.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:48.970
He hoped that the meal, a little glass of
wine, and the peaceful atmosphere would make

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:52.610
the Plague Doctor more amenable to his line
of questioning.

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:58.590
SCP-049 was sitting in front of the wide glass
window, fork and knife in hand, when Dr. Bradshaw

00:02:58.590 --> 00:02:59.590
arrived.

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:00.900
"Hello, Doctor."

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:02.730
Bradshaw gave 049 a wave.

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:03.920
"Ah, good evening!

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:07.170
They told me I would be having company for
dinner.

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:08.799
But where is your food, sir?"

00:03:08.799 --> 00:03:10.220
"Oh, I'm not hungry..."

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:12.970
Dr. Bradshaw brushed him off politely.

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:14.010
"Nonsense!

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:17.629
It would be horribly rude for me to eat while
you have nothing.

00:03:17.629 --> 00:03:18.730
Excuse me!"

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:20.549
He called to a guard nearby.

00:03:20.549 --> 00:03:25.349
"Please bring this man a helping of what you've
served me so we may dine together!"

00:03:25.349 --> 00:03:29.870
With a sigh, Dr. Bradshaw nodded at the guard,
giving him permission to do what the Plague

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:30.870
Doctor asked.

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:35.180
A few moments later, he too had a tray with
a plate of fish and roasted vegetables, and

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:37.489
a small cup of wine on the side.

00:03:37.489 --> 00:03:40.220
"Now, may I ask you some questions?"

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:41.760
The Plague Doctor nodded.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:42.760
"Indeed.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:44.340
What is it that I can do for you?"

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:47.879
"I was hoping you could teach me more about
The Pestilence..."

00:03:47.879 --> 00:03:50.370
Dr. Bradshaw began delicately.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:51.610
The Plague Doctor perked up.

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:54.250
"Have you come to assist me with my work?

00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:57.250
My dear fellow, why did you not say so from
the start?

00:03:57.250 --> 00:04:02.019
It would be my honor to provide you with all
the knowledge I possess on the subject, if

00:04:02.019 --> 00:04:03.910
you intend to lend a hand."

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:08.870
"I just want to understand more about it first,"
Dr. Bradshaw said.

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:11.360
It wasn't quite a lie, not really.

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:13.310
He did want to understand.

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:17.500
He flipped open his notebook, scanning the
page for the first of the posited theories

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:18.500
he had gathered.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:23.220
"Is it possible that when you refer to the
Pestilence, you're talking about pain as a

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:24.220
whole?"

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:26.360
The Plague Doctor cocked his head to the side.

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:28.040
"What do you mean?"

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:29.040
"Well.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:33.781
You once described SCP-096 as being afflicted
with an extreme case of The Pestilence, I

00:04:33.781 --> 00:04:34.781
believe.

00:04:34.781 --> 00:04:40.380
And when you...cure someone, they're no longer
able to feel any pain, physical or emotional.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:45.479
I know that you mean well, but is it possible
that's what you're trying to treat?

00:04:45.479 --> 00:04:49.560
The pain that naturally afflicts all human
life, most living things?"

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:50.919
The Plague Doctor scoffed.

00:04:50.919 --> 00:04:54.930
"You think that I, a physician, am so ignorant
of pain?

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:59.270
That I would misdiagnose agonies as something
so devastating as The Great Dying?

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:03.580
If you are here to insult me, sir, I will
simply ask that you leave."

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:06.090
"I'm sorry, that wasn't my intention.

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:08.130
Please, let me stay.

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:10.850
I promise I am only here to try and understand.

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:13.259
Will you withhold judgment until I'm finished?

00:05:13.259 --> 00:05:15.030
One man of science to another?"

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:17.290
The Plague Doctor paused, mulling this over.

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:18.290
He nodded.

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:21.419
"I will hear your questions and do my best
to answer them."

00:05:21.419 --> 00:05:22.419
"Thank you."

00:05:22.419 --> 00:05:24.990
Dr. Bradshaw smiled, and returned to his notes.

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:29.340
"So, you have been in the field of medicine
for quite some time, and I imagine you've

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:31.389
studied a variety of sciences.

00:05:31.389 --> 00:05:36.149
You work with a lot of liquids and substances
none of us have encountered before, were they

00:05:36.149 --> 00:05:37.870
the result of alchemy?

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:40.050
Used to balance the humors of your patients?

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:45.770
I was curious if your treatments, and if the
Pestilence itself, is thaumaturgical in nature.

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:48.010
That is to say, well...magical."

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:51.040
The Plague Doctor let out a sharp laugh at
this.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:52.240
"Magic?

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:55.150
Sir, I am no magician.

00:05:55.150 --> 00:06:00.310
Nor am I an alchemist, as I have never formally
studied alchemy in any form.

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:04.430
The humors, of course, must be balanced for
proper health, but that has nothing to do

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:06.630
with alchemy, in my opinion.

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:08.220
How is your black bile?

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:10.280
You appear to have an excess of it.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:13.039
Do you find you experience a great deal of
stomach pain?"

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:17.451
Dr. Bradshaw rushed to steer the topic away
from him and his health, lest the Plague Doctor

00:06:17.451 --> 00:06:19.470
offer to treat him.

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:21.139
"Not particularly.

00:06:21.139 --> 00:06:22.139
Next question..."

00:06:22.139 --> 00:06:23.970
His eyes returned to his notes.

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:28.160
"Let's see...We've noticed that you seem to
cure those who are most afraid of death and

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:29.160
dying.

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:32.979
And when you first arrived at our facility,
you stated that there was less Pestilence

00:06:32.979 --> 00:06:35.530
here than other places you had been.

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:40.020
Most of our staff have seen enough death and
misfortune that it no longer frightens us,

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:42.759
we're inoculated against it, essentially.

00:06:42.759 --> 00:06:46.620
One Researcher working with you was afraid
of death at first, but as he came to accept

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:50.610
it as an inevitability, you said that he was
no longer infected.

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:55.120
Is the Pestilence fear itself, or more specifically,
the fear of death?"

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:56.930
The Plague Doctor mulled this over.

00:06:56.930 --> 00:07:00.319
"One's outlook, one's attitude, can impact
their health.

00:07:00.319 --> 00:07:04.840
It is easier to fight a disease when one does
not let fear take over.

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:06.919
But other than that, I am afraid I do not
understand your question.

00:07:06.919 --> 00:07:10.910
The Pestilence is simply what it is.

00:07:10.910 --> 00:07:15.319
Why are you more preoccupied with categorizing
it than curing it?"

00:07:15.319 --> 00:07:20.330
"I believe that to understand something is
to know how to fight it," Dr. Bradshaw answered

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:21.330
simply.

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:22.919
"We may differ there.

00:07:22.919 --> 00:07:24.169
But you may continue.

00:07:24.169 --> 00:07:25.970
I am grateful for the company.

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:30.470
And for your desire to understand, misguided
though it may be."

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:31.680
"Thank you, Doctor.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:35.440
I wanted to ask you, are you familiar with
the Scarlet King?"

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.539
The Plague Doctor's expression did not change.

00:07:37.539 --> 00:07:43.320
"I have seen the rise and fall of many monarchs
in my time Why would you ask me about the

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:44.500
Scarlet King?"

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:46.260
That wasn't a "no."

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:51.770
"Well," Dr. Bradshaw began, "According to
legend, one of his many daughters was Pestilence,

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:54.380
a bringer of disease and destruction.

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:56.360
Is she the one you intend to fight?"

00:07:56.360 --> 00:08:02.919
"I know not every single form the Pestilence
takes, only the grip it has on this world,"

00:08:02.919 --> 00:08:04.000
SCP-049 answered.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:08.720
"For your sake, I would advise you to avoid
speaking of such matters.

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:12.620
That King...he is not a thing you wish to
face."

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:17.910
Dr. Bradshaw jotted down a quick note in the
margin of the page: "Scarlet King Connection

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:18.910
possible."

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:20.639
Then, he returned to his line of questioning.

00:08:20.639 --> 00:08:22.970
"Have you spoken with SCP-343?"

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:24.810
"I have."

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:26.040
The Plague Doctor nodded.

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:27.639
"And what did he tell you?"

00:08:27.639 --> 00:08:30.159
"It is not worth going into here..."

00:08:30.159 --> 00:08:34.430
"According to him, the Pestilence that you
refer to is humanity's free will.

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:39.750
You were changed by something a long time
ago, a tragedy, that turned you toward your

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:40.750
work.

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:44.890
You came from a dark place, one of the places
even 343 can't see.

00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:45.890
Is that true?"

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:47.750
"I will not answer that question.

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:51.029
It is an insult," SCP-049 said.

00:08:51.029 --> 00:08:53.360
Again, not a "no," exactly.

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:58.690
But still, Dr. Bradshaw did not want to push
too hard too soon and risk the Doctor terminating

00:08:58.690 --> 00:08:59.690
their conversation.

00:08:59.690 --> 00:09:00.950
"My apologies.

00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:02.890
I'll shift to something different.

00:09:02.890 --> 00:09:05.980
You once treated the bubonic plague, correct?"

00:09:05.980 --> 00:09:07.870
SCP-049 shook his head.

00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:11.950
"I am not familiar with that particular condition,
no."

00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:14.070
Dr. Bradshaw had prepared for this.

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:18.310
He pulled a medical ilustration from his notebook,
depicting a victim of The Black Plague.

00:09:18.310 --> 00:09:20.680
"What about something like this?"

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:22.170
SCP-049 sighed.

00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:27.120
"Ah, now this ailment I have encountered before.

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:30.120
I did my best to treat it, but...it was stubborn."

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:32.480
"Is it related to The Pestilence?"

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:34.420
Dr. Bradshaw asked.

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:35.990
"Not necessarily."

00:09:35.990 --> 00:09:38.220
049 said nothing more than that.

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:41.120
He took a sip of wine, ate a bite of potato.

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:45.620
He wasn't going to offer any more insight
into that particular theory, it seemed.

00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:51.100
"I mean no offense with this next question,
but Doctor...have you always lived here?

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:52.310
On this planet?

00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:56.790
Or did you make your way here from another
world of some kind?

00:09:56.790 --> 00:09:59.320
Perhaps the Pestilence came from wherever
you did."

00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:04.400
"The world has changed around me a great deal
in my life," SCP-049 said.

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:06.560
"But I have always been here.

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:09.560
At least, as best as I can recall."

00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:14.610
He seemed to be telling the truth, but with
that unmoving, inscrutable expression, it

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:16.240
was difficult to be sure.

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:21.240
This next theory would be extremely difficult
to float without offending SCP-049, but Dr.

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:22.890
Bradshaw had to try.

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:24.790
He needed to do his due diligence.

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:28.670
"Are you familiar with the story of Typhoid
Mary?"

00:10:28.670 --> 00:10:31.610
He would ease into the idea, that would be
best.

00:10:31.610 --> 00:10:32.990
"I am not."

00:10:32.990 --> 00:10:34.860
SCP-049 sounded puzzled.

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:39.709
"She was an asymptomatic carrier of Typhoid,"
Dr. Bradshaw explained.

00:10:39.709 --> 00:10:42.690
"As others grew sick around her, she remained
healthy and well.

00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:47.530
She had no idea that, as she worked as a cook
in the houses of wealthy families, she was

00:10:47.530 --> 00:10:49.820
infecting them all with a deadly disease.

00:10:49.820 --> 00:10:53.980
It was not any moral failing of her own, she
didn't understand what she was doing, but

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:56.079
she still spread the virus throughout her
community-"

00:10:56.079 --> 00:10:58.590
"Stop right there."

00:10:58.590 --> 00:11:01.250
The Plague Doctor's tone was stern, tense.

00:11:01.250 --> 00:11:05.040
"I know what you are suggesting, and it offends
me to my core.

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:08.950
If you do not cease these vile implications
I will be forced to end our talk here and

00:11:08.950 --> 00:11:09.950
now.

00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:14.589
I am a physician, sir, I do not endanger my
patients so recklessly."

00:11:14.589 --> 00:11:16.690
"I understand."

00:11:16.690 --> 00:11:18.600
Better to leave that theory alone for now.

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:23.560
"You said that you have always been here,
but I do have to ask...have you ever heard

00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:25.380
of a place called Alagadda?

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:29.800
There are those that claim you once lived
there, that you came from that kingdom."

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:33.070
"I have never heard that name, that I can
recall.

00:11:33.070 --> 00:11:37.740
But I have lived a long life, perhaps I have
forgotten.

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:39.450
What nation does it belong to?"

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:40.740
Dr. Bradshaw paused.

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:43.110
It was his turn to be stumped.

00:11:43.110 --> 00:11:44.930
"I'm not sure.

00:11:44.930 --> 00:11:47.209
Do you know who The Black Lord is?"

00:11:47.209 --> 00:11:51.060
He couldn't be certain, but he thought he
saw SCP-049 frown.

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:52.060
"Villain!

00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:54.959
A villain," he grumbled under his breath.

00:11:54.959 --> 00:11:59.180
"I banish him from my thoughts, that he may
never again enter my sight."

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:04.150
His gloved hands clenched into fists, and
Dr. Bradshow jotted down another note in the

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:05.150
margins.

00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:06.150
The Black Lord.

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:10.400
He had devoted plenty of time to everyone
else's theories, it was time for one that

00:12:10.400 --> 00:12:12.850
he had been earnestly considering.

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:18.800
"Doctor, it is my opinion that the Pestilence
may be connected to emotional state or heart

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:20.410
rate, perhaps both.

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:24.810
When you treat your patients, do you remove
the heart or the amygdala to address those

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:25.810
issues?

00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:28.300
I have noticed heart removal during a handful
of your procedures."

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:30.160
"You have a keen eye!"

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:31.660
SCP-049 enthused.

00:12:31.660 --> 00:12:34.550
"However, I do not always remove the heart.

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:37.500
It varies greatly from patient to patient,
you see.

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:40.019
For some, the heart is a crucial part of the
infection.

00:12:40.019 --> 00:12:42.839
For others, it impacts the brain.

00:12:42.839 --> 00:12:45.200
Others still, the liver or the larger nervous
system.

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:48.389
That is why it is so difficult to treat, you
see.

00:12:48.389 --> 00:12:51.839
It can manifest in seemingly infinite ways."

00:12:51.839 --> 00:12:54.079
"But what about emotions?

00:12:54.079 --> 00:12:55.079
Negative impulses?

00:12:55.079 --> 00:12:56.500
Cruelty, selfishness, hatred.

00:12:56.500 --> 00:12:58.709
Is that a component?

00:12:58.709 --> 00:13:02.820
I was reviewing your file, and you mentioned
an excess of the Pestilence in the home of

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:07.190
a wealthy man known for exploiting his workers
and mistreating his famiy.

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:11.980
When you were cross-tested with SCP-682 you
remarked that the Pestilence did not only

00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:15.680
impact humans, suggesting the anomaly was
also infected.

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:21.430
And your cured patients, well, they don't
seem to have a hateful impulse in their bodies."

00:13:21.430 --> 00:13:23.980
"Hate...can be a disease."

00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:26.540
The Plague Doctor took another bite of food,
chewing slowly.

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:30.730
When he swallowed, Dr. Bradsaw expected him
to elaborate on this thought.

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:32.080
But he didn't.

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:33.080
"So..."

00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:34.270
Dr. Bradshaw broke the silence.

00:13:34.270 --> 00:13:38.630
"The four horsemen of the apocalypse, the
ones that bring about the end of the world.

00:13:38.630 --> 00:13:40.950
War, famine, death, and...pestilence.

00:13:40.950 --> 00:13:44.709
There have been stories about them for thousands
of years.

00:13:44.709 --> 00:13:47.250
Do you know Pestilence personally?

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:50.260
Are you protecting us from him, from the end
of days?"

00:13:50.260 --> 00:13:51.490
SCP-049 scoffed.

00:13:51.490 --> 00:13:55.370
"I work in science, not in stories.

00:13:55.370 --> 00:14:00.870
I intend no disrespect to your beliefs, but
surely you hear how absurd that sounds.

00:14:00.870 --> 00:14:03.190
Pestilence does not ride in on a horse.

00:14:03.190 --> 00:14:05.190
It resides in the body."

00:14:05.190 --> 00:14:07.880
"You've lost patients before, haven't you?"

00:14:07.880 --> 00:14:11.399
"Every physician has, if what they are treating
is severe.

00:14:11.399 --> 00:14:13.680
It never gets any easier to do so."

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:14.680
"I'm sure.

00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:17.079
Have you ever lost someone special to you,
though?

00:14:17.079 --> 00:14:19.360
Done something you truly regret?"

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:23.699
Perhaps the Pestilence was a manifestation
of some guilt or trauma, some shame he could

00:14:23.699 --> 00:14:25.540
never truly let go.

00:14:25.540 --> 00:14:27.640
"Every man has regrets.

00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:30.639
Do you not have those secret shames, Doctor?"

00:14:30.639 --> 00:14:32.380
Dr. Bradshaw changed the subject.

00:14:32.380 --> 00:14:34.320
"I have always wondered this.

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:36.670
Can you see the misdeeds that someone has
done?

00:14:36.670 --> 00:14:41.199
Can you somehow sense the sins of their past,
all the harm they have caused?"

00:14:41.199 --> 00:14:43.360
"Why do you ask?"

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:44.720
SCP-049 cocked his head again.

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:47.490
"Are you afraid I can see something within
you?"

00:14:47.490 --> 00:14:50.959
He couldn't explain why, but the question
made his stomach turn.

00:14:50.959 --> 00:14:51.959
"No."

00:14:51.959 --> 00:14:52.959
He answered.

00:14:52.959 --> 00:14:53.959
It was a lie.

00:14:53.959 --> 00:14:55.810
"Will everyone catch the Pestilence eventually?"

00:14:55.810 --> 00:14:59.649
"As time goes on, the odds do become greater,
yes.

00:14:59.649 --> 00:15:00.870
But not everyone is doomed."

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:03.000
"Is that it?

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:04.079
Is it time?

00:15:04.079 --> 00:15:05.870
The passage of time, aging, decay.

00:15:05.870 --> 00:15:07.910
The Great Dying, you called it.

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:10.800
So...is it dying, itself?

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:12.620
Your cured patients, they don't age.

00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:15.520
They'll never decay any more than they already
have."

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:19.980
"Death is not natural," SCP-049 simply said.

00:15:19.980 --> 00:15:21.490
"All things die eventually."

00:15:21.490 --> 00:15:22.920
Dr. Bradshaw argued.

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:25.510
"I have not died yet."

00:15:25.510 --> 00:15:27.130
SCP-049 took a drink of wine.

00:15:27.130 --> 00:15:29.330
Well, there was no arguing with that.

00:15:29.330 --> 00:15:32.850
"Something else I've been thinking about,
is the possibility that the Pestilence is

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:34.670
greater than just one thing."

00:15:34.670 --> 00:15:37.030
Dr. Bradshaw began his next theory.

00:15:37.030 --> 00:15:41.560
"Doctors believed at one time that the Plague
was caused by an imbalance of the humors in

00:15:41.560 --> 00:15:42.560
the body.

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:48.019
They could recognize the presence of disease,
but couldn't routinely tell them all apart.

00:15:48.019 --> 00:15:51.850
Several different illnesses were referred
to as Consumption, and Leprosy was used as

00:15:51.850 --> 00:15:54.430
a catch-all for multiple illnesses as well.

00:15:54.430 --> 00:15:58.250
Clearly, you have a knack for knowing when
someone is ill, but what if it's something

00:15:58.250 --> 00:15:59.350
else?

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:02.810
Something equally bad, but different from
what you think?

00:16:02.810 --> 00:16:05.509
It's cancer, or a stroke, or a heart condition.

00:16:05.509 --> 00:16:10.029
There is no one cure, except for the massive
transformational work you're already doing."

00:16:10.029 --> 00:16:12.180
"Possibly," SCP-049 mused.

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:16.209
"I will need to consider that point carefully.

00:16:16.209 --> 00:16:20.459
Perhaps you have unlocked a secret to the
Pestilence I missed.

00:16:20.459 --> 00:16:21.990
Then again, perhaps not."

00:16:21.990 --> 00:16:25.550
"If the Pestilence is not death...then I do
have to wonder.

00:16:25.550 --> 00:16:26.890
Is it the opposite?

00:16:26.890 --> 00:16:31.620
Life is a temporary state for so many of us
less lucky than you, and it seems like you

00:16:31.620 --> 00:16:34.750
cure your patients of natural life when you
treat them.

00:16:34.750 --> 00:16:39.890
They become something not quite dead but not
quite living, at least not as they were.

00:16:39.890 --> 00:16:44.610
And illness, ailments, hurt, all comes as
a natural side effect of being alive.

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:47.910
So is the Great Dying...life itself?"

00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:54.550
"I do not understand the question, and thus,
I will not respond to it," SCP-049 stated.

00:16:54.550 --> 00:16:58.949
Dr. Bradshaw could sense that The Plague Doctor
was growing frustrated, getting tired of so

00:16:58.949 --> 00:17:03.530
many questions that poked at his beliefs,
his research, his process.

00:17:03.530 --> 00:17:06.660
"Is the Pestilence natural, or something manmade?

00:17:06.660 --> 00:17:11.280
Humans have created diseases before, caused
them to mutate, or worsened them with chemicals

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:12.280
we invent.

00:17:12.280 --> 00:17:13.900
Have you heard of Agent Orange?

00:17:13.900 --> 00:17:18.329
I don't mean to digress, but my point is,
it's not unheard of for people to be responsible

00:17:18.329 --> 00:17:19.780
for a disease.

00:17:19.780 --> 00:17:23.750
I wonder...is the Pestilence something that
developed on its own?

00:17:23.750 --> 00:17:25.080
Or is it our doing?

00:17:25.080 --> 00:17:30.929
A biological weapon, a synthesized virus or
artificial mutation created by other scientists?

00:17:30.929 --> 00:17:31.929
Or maybe..."

00:17:31.929 --> 00:17:35.450
Dr. Bradshaw paused here, referring once more
to his notes.

00:17:35.450 --> 00:17:36.520
"Is it sin?

00:17:36.520 --> 00:17:42.690
Greed, lust, wrath, pride, envy, gluttony,
sloth...maybe not that biblical, that exact,

00:17:42.690 --> 00:17:44.669
but is it evil?

00:17:44.669 --> 00:17:47.780
The sort of thing that rots away at the human
soul?

00:17:47.780 --> 00:17:51.669
Not a plague of the body, but of the very
essence, and the only way to cure it is to

00:17:51.669 --> 00:17:54.039
rip the whole thing out and start over?"

00:17:54.039 --> 00:17:58.480
"You seem to have the answers in mind before
you ask me these questions, Doctor.

00:17:58.480 --> 00:18:03.210
I will allow you one more before I take my
leave, I have found this whole ordeal quite

00:18:03.210 --> 00:18:04.210
exhausting."

00:18:04.210 --> 00:18:06.010
Dr. Bradshaw nodded.

00:18:06.010 --> 00:18:07.750
"Okay, just one more.

00:18:07.750 --> 00:18:13.100
Is it something unnatural, not manmade, not
of nature, viruses or bacteria, but something

00:18:13.100 --> 00:18:14.799
else altogether?

00:18:14.799 --> 00:18:19.130
Something from another dimension, another
world, or the spaces in between.

00:18:19.130 --> 00:18:22.860
You have been cagey about the nature of the
Pestilence for as long as you have been here,

00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:27.341
and I understand if you are just being cautious,
but...I can't help but wonder if there is

00:18:27.341 --> 00:18:31.010
something else to the reason you keep so much
to yourself.

00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:34.472
You promised to tell me about the Pestilence,
but you've barely responded to most of my

00:18:34.472 --> 00:18:36.730
questions except to dismiss my ideas.

00:18:36.730 --> 00:18:40.390
Is the Pestilence something that spreads by
thought?

00:18:40.390 --> 00:18:44.909
That lives in the consciousness, or the subconscious,
of us all?

00:18:44.909 --> 00:18:49.820
Waiting, dormant, for something to activate
it and set the sickness free?"

00:18:49.820 --> 00:18:52.150
The Plague Doctor set down his fork with a
clatter.

00:18:52.150 --> 00:18:57.870
"I respect your scientific curiosity, but
there are things I cannot share with you.

00:18:57.870 --> 00:19:01.600
For my safety, for yours, for everyone's.

00:19:01.600 --> 00:19:03.160
Do take care, Doctor."

00:19:03.160 --> 00:19:04.200
"You too."

00:19:04.200 --> 00:19:07.580
Dr. Bradshaw stood from his chair, collecting
his notes and preparing to leave.

00:19:07.580 --> 00:19:08.980
"One final thing..."

00:19:08.980 --> 00:19:12.060
Dr. Bradshaw turned back to face the Plague
Doctor.

00:19:12.060 --> 00:19:13.060
"Yes?"

00:19:13.060 --> 00:19:15.630
Those dark eyes watched him from inside the
mask.

00:19:15.630 --> 00:19:19.290
"Do you see any of the Pestilence in me?"

00:19:19.290 --> 00:19:22.050
The silence that passed between them felt
like an eternity.

00:19:22.050 --> 00:19:23.050
Then…

00:19:23.050 --> 00:19:24.380
SCP-049 spoke.

00:19:24.380 --> 00:19:25.930
"No, sir.

00:19:25.930 --> 00:19:27.410
I do not.

00:19:27.410 --> 00:19:30.539
But one day, that could change."

00:19:30.539 --> 00:19:35.409
As Dr. Bradshaw made his way back to his office,
the words echoed in his mind, and he couldn't

00:19:35.409 --> 00:19:38.190
make up his mind whether they were comforting,
or troubling.

00:19:38.190 --> 00:19:43.500
He had gone in to the meeting with questions,
but now, he had even more than he started

00:19:43.500 --> 00:19:44.500
with.

